s 3$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding $3$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $2\\times 3$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $AB$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-18 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 42 & 0 & -21 \\\\ 8 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329640 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186451263 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186329640 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186451264 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186329640 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186451265 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186329640 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42264" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329640 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329640 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $2\\times 2$ matrices, which of the following statements are always true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $AB=BA$\n\n- **II.** $(AB)C=A(BC)$\n\n- **III.** $A(B-C)=(B-C)A$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186329640 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "005a0156-3d99-59d1-ab99-adf9b9b4c7b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329640 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C=A(BC)$ in general.\n\n- Statement III is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $A(B-C)\\ne (B-C)A$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345115 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452661 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186345115 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452662 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186345115 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452663 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186345115 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75662" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345115 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345115 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $7\\times 7$ matrices, which of the following statements are always true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A(B+C)=AC+AB$\n- **II.** $A(BC)=(BA)C$\n- **III.** $AB=BA$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186345115 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1c88851c-8567-55d2-897e-8b0f9b020c35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345115 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so \n\n$$\nA(B+C)=AB+AC=AC+AB\n$$\n\n since matrix addition is commutative.\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C=A(BC)$ in general. However, since matrix multiplication is not commutative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(BC)=(AB)C\\ne (BA)C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345117 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452667 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186345117 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452668 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186345117 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452669 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186345117 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75717" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345117 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345117 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $6\\times 6$ matrices which of the following statements are always true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $CB=BC$\n- **II.** $A(B-C)=AB-CA$\n- **III.** $A(BC)=(AB)C$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186345117 #edb/ref 107